00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:18.541
The shift follows comparisons with SpaceX's recent webcast.

00:02:18.541 --> 00:02:24.410
The economics of putting AI training and inference in orbit rest on a narrow set of physical

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:27.330
advantages that still have to clear steep cost hurdles.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.870
Cooling in vacuum is physically simpler than on Earth, and a T.S. Alamming post argued

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.890
that is the part of orbital data centers critics get wrong.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.270
Radiative heat rejection works efficiently in vacuum.

00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:44.055
The post covers redundant pumping loops and micrometeoroid shielding.

00:02:44.495 --> 00:02:48.675
It also flags Earth's infrared glow along with coatings that wear down in orbit.

00:02:48.675 --> 00:02:54.295
An ABI estimate states that a GPU year in space costs at least 10 times more today.

00:02:54.975 --> 00:02:59.595
The post never claimed orbital data centers are already cheaper. The government accountability

00:02:59.595 --> 00:03:05.595
office or GAO finds the underlying power, cooling, and communications technologies are mature.

00:03:06.295 --> 00:03:12.290
Scale at data center level remains unproven. Absence of gravity-driven convection allows certain

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:15.570
crystal growth processes to run without terrestrial interference.

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:22.470
coatings also degrade under atomic oxygen, and those extras multiply mass and therefore launch expense.

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:27.890
Every kilogram saved directly reduces the price of each GPU year delivered in space.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:34.910
SpaceX's Starfall capsule is now offered commercially with up to 1,000 kilograms of payload capacity

00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.170
and continuous starlink connectivity.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:43.090
Customers in biomedical and semiconductor fields can run experiments for weeks rather than seconds.

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:46.570
They pull data in real-time instead of waiting for recovery.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.810
The capsule's 1 kilowatt power limit and Ethernet interface set clear boundaries on what

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:52.710
experiments fit.

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:57.850
Launching on Starship keeps cadence and cost per kilogram as the decisive variables.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:03.910
The commercial page lists three target markets, biomedical-organoid growth, pharmaceutical

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.450
crystal formation, and material science for next-generation semiconductors.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:12.010
Each mission supplies power and data links throughout the flight.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:16.410
That removes the need for post-splashdown retrieval before analysis begins.

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:21.950
For Tesla, the relevant question is whether orbital compute ever becomes cheaper than terrestrial

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:24.450
clusters for FSD and Optimus training.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:30.430
If launch costs fall faster than terrestrial electricity and land prices rise, the crossover

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:31.690
point moves closer.

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:36.310
If shielding and redundancy costs stay high, the advantage stays theoretical.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:41.570
The outcome will shape how much of Tesla's future inference load stays on Earth versus moving

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:47.390
off-planet. Those starfall caps already define the practical envelope for any early orbital node.

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:53.110
The same constraints that make cooling easier also cap how large a single payload can grow before

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:58.670
multiple launches become necessary. The balance between those limits and starships projected flight

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:04.230
rate will determine whether orbital AI infrastructure moves from concept to production capacity within

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.130
in the next decade.

